{
  "text": " It seems like you and I have something in common, which is we never get an unfriendly interview. We're never on CNN. We're never interviewed by the New York Times. Like, it never happens to me. I bet it never happens to you, right? That you got to talk to the opposition. They all just want to talk to a Neco Chamber, right? Yeah, not on film or audio. You know, and that's one of the problems is that there's always polarization in our country. If we're going to end it, and the polarization is really dangerous, we're probably more polarized in this country than any time in American history, except in probably five to seven years preceding the Civil War. And it's really unclear how we can get out of this, but we're definitely not going to get out of it if we don't start talking to people with whom we disagree. That's right. And the worst thing that you can do to people is censorship. If number one, it doesn't work, it doesn't stop the idea, it doesn't kill the ideas. People, as you know, and I know, find ways of talking with each other. And the resentments grow and the anger grows, that you're not being heard, that there is no forum. And, you know, and democracy functions on the free flow of information. Debate, conversations, dialogue, disagreements are sunlight and fertilizer to democracy without it. Without those things, democracy will wither and die.",
